Equilibrium constants can be expressed in terms of pressures as $K_p$ or in terms of concentrations as $K_c$. This is because both pressure and concentration are related to the amount of matter; that is, the more concentrated the solution, the larger the amount of reactant when the reaction happens in liquid solution. Similarly, the higher the pressure, the larger the amount of reactant when the reaction happens in the gas phase, in gas mixtures. 
There is a general way to refer to the amount of matter called the \emph{activity}. Activities $a$ not only unify the different ways of expressing the amount of matter, but also take into account deviations from ideal behavior when intermolecular interactions are relevant. 

The concept of activity of a chemical was coined by G. N. Lewis in 1907, and is an advanced topic covered in physical chemistry classes. By convention, activities are dimensionless quantities, defined with respect to customary choices of standard states. The activity of pure substances in condensed phases (solids and liquids) $a^*$ is taken as one. The activity of a chemical in solution or in a gas mixture is defined as:

  \begin{equation}
\boxed{a^*_{(l)}=1}  \;\;\;\;\; \text{and}\;\;\;\;\;  \boxed{  a^*_{(s)}=1} 
\end{equation}

The definition of activity contains a coefficient called the \emph{activity coefficient} $\gamma$, which quantifies the strength of the intermolecular forces acting between the molecules of the chemical. The weaker this forces the close would this coeficient be to 1 and hence concentrations (or pressures) would equal to activities. But this equality only holds when the intermolecular interactions are weak.

 
 \begin{equation}
\boxed{
a_{(aq)}=\gamma\frac{c}{c^* }
  \;\;\;\;\;\;
  a_{(g)}=\gamma\frac{p}{p^* }
}
\end{equation}

All equilibrium constants are originally written in terms of activities, and as such are unitless magnitudes. The general definition of an equilibrium constant in terms of activities is given by
\begin{equation}
\boxed{  K= \frac{a_{\text{Products}_{(aq)}}}{a_{\text{Reactants}_{(aq)}}}
%=\frac{\gamma_{\text{Products}}}{\gamma_{\text{Reactants}}}\cdot  \frac{   \frac{c_{\text{Products}}}{a^*_{\text{Products}}}   }{   \frac{c_{\text{Reactants}}}{a^*_{\text{Reactants}}}   }
%=\frac{\big[ C \big]^c \cdot \big[ D \big]^d} {\big[ A \big]^a \cdot \big[ B \big]^b} } \quad \textcolor{blue}{\text{Equilibrium constant}}\label{\chapterlabel:equation1
}
\end{equation}

Using chemical activities instead of raw concentrations or partial pressures makes the equilibrium constant completely unitless and thermodynamically rigorous.